# =============================================================================
# Nova CRM — environment variables (EXAMPLE — placeholders only, never secrets)
# =============================================================================
# Local development:  copy this file to `.env` and adjust the values.
#                     (`.env` is gitignored — it never leaves your machine.)
# Production (Vercel): set the same variables in
#                     Project → Settings → Environment Variables.
# Full step-by-step: see DEPLOYMENT.md.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development uses SQLite (no server needed):
#   DATABASE_URL="file:./db/custom.db"
#
# Production uses Supabase Postgres. DATABASE_URL must be the TRANSACTION
# POOLER connection string (port 6543) — Vercel's serverless functions must not
# open direct connections. `pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1` are REQUIRED
# with transaction pooling (one connection per serverless instance).
# Note: use the `postgres` (table owner) credentials here — see DEPLOYMENT.md
# §2 for why (the *_owner_admin RLS policies grant the app full access).
# TIP: if you forget ?pgbouncer=true the app now fixes the URL in memory at
# runtime (src/lib/db.ts) — but keep the params in the env var anyway.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"

# After changing any env var, verify the deployment with:
#   https://<your-domain>/api/health
# It shows whether the database is reachable, which Prisma client was
# generated, and classified reasons (with fixes) when something is wrong.

# Direct (non-pooled) Postgres connection, port 5432. Used by
# prisma/schema.postgres.prisma (directUrl) for migration/introspection tooling
# and one-off scripts. In the Supabase dashboard this is the
# "Connection string → URI" under Settings → Database (Session mode also works).
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# ── App ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public URL of the deployment (no trailing slash). Used to build email links
# (team invitation accept links, welcome email CTA). Set it to your final
# production URL after the first deploy — e.g. https://zcrm11.vercel.app or
# your custom domain — then redeploy.
# Resolution order: APP_URL → the request's own origin (x-forwarded-host) →
# http://localhost:3000. So even with APP_URL unset, invite links generated on
# a Vercel deployment point at that deployment's domain.
APP_URL="https://zcrm11.vercel.app"

# ── Email (Resend — transactional email) ─────────────────────────────────────
# API key from resend.com/api-keys. Without it the app runs in "demo mode":
# invitations succeed but the accept link is shown in the UI (copy-link
# fallback) instead of being emailed. Never throw-causes a request.
RESEND_API_KEY="re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"

# Sender identity for outbound email. Must be an address on a domain you have
# VERIFIED in Resend (Domains → Add domain → add the SPF/DKIM DNS records).
# The default `onboarding@resend.dev` only delivers to the Resend account
# owner's own inbox — fine for testing, not for production.
MAIL_FROM="Nova CRM <noreply@yourdomain.com>"

# ── Integrations (no env vars) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# All integrations — WhatsApp Business, GitHub, Slack, Stripe, Resend, Supabase,
# … — are configured PER WORKSPACE inside the app: Settings → Integrations.
# Credentials are stored in the database per organization (secrets are masked
# in API responses). There are deliberately no WHATSAPP_* env vars; see
# DEPLOYMENT.md §4 for the WhatsApp Business setup (Meta developer app →
# phone number ID + permanent system-user token → paste into the UI).
#
# Not read by the current code (reserved for the optional Supabase
# PostgREST/supabase-js client path described in migrations/README.md §3):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""
#
# NODE_ENV is set automatically by Next.js — nothing to configure.
